"How to Fight a Traffic Ticket in El Paso Texas (20 Steps)": "",
"Here are some steps you can use to fight an": "",
"El Paso Police traffic ticket": "",
"yourself. The process is time consuming but can be done — or you can always": "",
"hire a traffic ticket lawyer": "",
"to do it for you.": "",
"Be polite and cooperative when you get pulled over.": "",
"Being belligerent may make you feel better but it might cost you more. By being polite, the officer may write your ticket for a less costly offense — or you might just get a warning. If you are nasty or curt, the officer may note it, and prosecutors will be less likely to cut you a deal.": "",
"Avoid admissions of guilt and never make excuses or outlandish stories.": "",
"When asked if you know why you were pulled over, respond with a simple, polite \"No officer, I do not.\" Any admissions you make now can be used against you later.": "",
"Decide how you will interact with the officer.": "",
"The 'low-profile' approach: ask if you can handle the ticket by mail, so the officer sees you as unlikely to contest it and takes fewer notes. Alternatively, politely ask how the offense was detected — for speeding, where the officer was positioned and what device was used (radar, laser or pacing) — and note the patrol car's plate and badge number. The officer does not have to give device details at the stop; you can request them later through discovery.": "",
"Check your ticket for accuracy immediately.": "",
"If there are inaccuracies that hurt your case, politely ask the officer to correct them; if not accommodated, write down what actually happened after the stop. If there are inaccuracies that help you (wrong plate, wrong street), do not call attention to them.": "",
"Begin preparing your defense immediately.": "",
"Record traffic, road conditions, weather, time of day and extenuating circumstances. Take photos — especially of an obscured sign or a hazard you had to avoid. A dash-cam is the best witness you can have. Check the officer's vantage point for obstructions and draw a diagram of the scene.": "",
"Read the fine print on the ticket at home.": "",
"It tells you the court, your appearance date and how to proceed.": "",
"Decide whether to fight.": "",
"Look up the exact offense charged by the code on the ticket. Weigh the cost of a conviction — the fine, any programs, and years of higher insurance — against the cost of contesting it.": "",
"Calculate the cost of fighting versus the chance of dismissal or reduction.": "",
"Decide whether you need a lawyer.": "",
"In El Paso Class C cases an attorney can appear for you so you never go to court. For many people the attorney's flat fee is less than the insurance increase a conviction causes.": "",
"Request a trial — do not just pay.": "",
"In Texas, paying the fine is a plea of guilty or no contest and becomes a conviction. Follow the steps on the ticket to enter a not-guilty plea and get your day in court.": "",
"Get as much information as you can.": "",
"Well before your court date, send a written request for discovery — the officer's copy of the ticket, calibration and maintenance records for any speed-measuring device, and the officer's training and certifications.": "",
"Try to work out a deal.": "",
"Many courts allow a pretrial conference with the prosecutor where deferred disposition, a driving safety course or a reduced charge can be negotiated. Understand the effect of any deal on your record and insurance before accepting.": "",
"Consider a driving safety course.": "",
"If eligible (see the": "",
"TDLR rules": "",
"and our": "",
"defensive driving guide": "",
"), completing an approved course gets the case dismissed. Commercial drivers are not eligible.": "",
"Request a continuance if needed.": "",
"The officer generally must appear at trial. A reset increases the chance of a no-show, which can lead to dismissal.": "",
"Plan your defense.": "",
"The State must prove every element: identifying the driver, the location, the signs or signals, the speed measurement. If an element is missing and you catch it, the case may be dismissed. Minor discrepancies (the color of your car) will not help; extenuating circumstances like 'I was late for work' will not impress the judge. Outline your points and organize your evidence.": "",
"Go to court and plead not guilty.": "",
"Dress cleanly and professionally. If you have not spoken to the prosecutor, do so. Unless a satisfactory deal is offered, plead not guilty. If the officer fails to appear, ask for dismissal.": "",
"Present facts without admitting guilt.": "",
"'I was only doing 57 in a 55' is an admission. 'I was traveling at a safe speed for the conditions' is not. Politely and clearly explain your defense and enter your evidence.": "",
"Note the judge's decision.": "",
"Many Class C proceedings are not recorded. Make sure what the clerk enters matches what the judge ordered.": "",
"Follow through with every court requirement.": "",
"Deferred disposition and driving safety course dismissals only help if you complete every condition on time. Miss a deadline and the conviction is entered.": "",
"Request a copy of your driving record afterward.": "",
"Clerical errors can cause a dismissed ticket to appear as a conviction. Order your record from": "",
"Texas DPS": "",
"and verify the case shows as dismissed.": "",
"The procedures above are governed largely by": "",
"Chapter 45 of the Texas Code of Criminal Procedure": "",
"(justice and municipal courts). If this looks like more than you want to take on, our office does all of it for a flat fee and you never have to appear. Watch the short video on our": "",
